<resources>

    <!-- 🔴 THE 5 GHz HOTSPOT FIX. Without this the SoftAP silently falls back to
         2.4 GHz: the band saves in Settings and snaps back the moment the
         hotspot starts.

         AOSP's own default is FALSE
         (packages/modules/Wifi/service/ServiceWifiResources/res/values/config.xml:31)
         and a device with a 5 GHz radio is expected to overlay it. We never did,
         so we inherited it — the same shape as config_defaultRefreshRate
         inheriting 60 and clamping a 120 Hz panel (v2 build 1).

         THE DEFECT IS AN ASYMMETRY, and it is why 5 GHz scanning has always
         worked while the AP never has. Two consumers read this boolean and only
         one of them has a fallback:

           WifiServiceImpl:4392  is5GhzBandSupportedInternal()
             if (config_wifi5ghzSupport) return true;
             return mWifiNative.getChannelsForBand(WIFI_BAND_5_GHZ).length > 0;
                                                   ^ ASKS THE DRIVER, so 5 GHz
                                                     scanning has always worked

           ApConfigUtil:1015     isSoftApBandSupported(BAND_5GHZ)
             return config_wifi5ghzSupport (AND) config_wifiSoftap5ghzSupported;
                    ^ FALSE                       ^ already true
             no driver fallback at all

         So the framework refuses the band before it ever asks the driver, while
         the driver is at that same moment enumerating eight non-DFS 5 GHz
         channels. Measured on v2 build 2:

           wificond: 5Ghz non-DFS frequencies: 5180 5200 5220 5240 5745 5765 5785 5805
           wificond: 5Ghz DFS frequencies:     5260 5280 5300 5320
           E ApConfigUtil: Can not start softAp with band 5Ghz not supported.
           SoftApInfo{... frequency= 2437 ...}          (channel 6)
           D WifiTetherApBandPref: Updating band index to 0   (the UI snapping back)

         Control, in the same log: 6 GHz emits the SAME message (both booleans
         false, and correct, because there is no 6 GHz radio), and 2.4 GHz emits
         nothing (both true). Three bands, three outcomes, one mechanism.

         ⚠ Setting this true has exactly ONE behavioural consequence. The other
         consumer, is5GHzBandSupported(), already returned true through its
         driver fallback, so this only moves it to the fast path. Verified by
         reading both call sites rather than assuming. -->
    <bool name="config_wifi5ghzSupport">true</bool>

    <!-- NOT changed, deliberately, so this is not re-litigated.
         config_wifi6ghzSupport stays false: there is no 6 GHz radio, and its
         "not supported" line in the log is CORRECT.
         config_wifiSoftapAcsIncludeDfs stays false: DFS (5260-5320) needs radar
         detection, so excluding it from auto channel selection is the safe
         default and is not what was broken here. -->

</resources>
